    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d573">

                <pb facs="0725" n="601" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg601"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/2206: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The President of the Technical Board</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Stevens</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Harbin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-08-16T12:00:00+08:26"><hi rendition="#italic">August 16,
                                1921—noon</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received 1:47 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Hayakawa, the new president of South Manchuria Railway, has just visited Harbin.
                    Elaborate entertainments which are always the preliminary to intrigues in the
                    Far East. Strongly urged me to approve change of gauge Changchun-Harbin to South
                    Manchuria gauge, to which I replied would require serious consideration. This
                    matter was up in the year 1919 but I succeeded in quashing as the files of the
                    Department will show;<anchor n="34" xml:id="d573-f1"/> such a proposition must
                    not be entertained. I anticipate Japan will seek to make it a condition of
                    negotiations with Allies regarding Technical Board and financiering. … He
                    [Hayakawa] has been given unlimited powers to decide tactics main railway line
                    and the Army ordered to show absolute submission to him.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stevens</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d573-f1" n="34" xml:id="d573-f1-content">
                <p>See telegrams of Apr. 17, 1919, from the Ambassador in Japan, and of Aug. 11,
                    1919, from the President of the Technical Board, <ref
                        target="frus1919v01#pg_606"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1919, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, pp. 606</ref> and <ref
                        target="frus1919v01#pg_612">612</ref>, respectively.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
